Extended Reality (XR) integrates physical and digital environments through continuous multimodal sensing, creating tension between privacy protection and user experience (UX): strong privacy controls enhance safety but disrupt immersion; seamless interaction obscures how systems sense, infer, and derive sensitive insights from personal data. This Systematization of Knowledge (SoK) examines these tensions (i.e., trade-offs) through a socio-technical systems (STS) lens spanning technical, social, and environmental dimensions. From a curated corpus of 51 XR-usable privacy studies, we construct a three-dimensional taxonomy of 10 themes and 42 factors that capture design choices and system or user behaviors shaping privacy-UX trade-offs across the reviewed literature. Our analysis reveals recurring tensions around transparency, control, trust, fairness, accountability, and contextual awareness, which together motivate five research challenges and 11 research directions. By linking the themes identified in our corpus to cross-cutting challenges and research directions, this work outlines an analytically grounded agenda for future efforts to better balance privacy protection and UX in immersive systems.